  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "If $c=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2k \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],$ $d=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ and $c\u22a5d,$ then $k=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "We know that $c\u22a5d$ ($c$ and $d$ are perpendicular) if and only if $c\\cdot d=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & ={c}_{x}\\cdot {d}_{x}+{c}_{y}\\cdot {d}_{y}+{c}_{z}\\cdot {d}_{z} \\\\  & =k\\cdot (-3)+2k\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-3k+4k+8 \\\\  & =k+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & =0 \\\\ k+8 & =0 \\\\ k & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
